# Components the image needs that the base distribution does not package.
#
# Split out from hcs-core.list.chroot on purpose. `verify_package_availability.py`
# checks that every name in the apt list actually exists in the target suite, and
# a name that has to come from somewhere else would make that check lie in the
# other direction — reporting a package as missing when the real answer is "this
# one is provisioned differently, here is how".
#
# Each entry declares where it comes from. The build refuses to treat an entry as
# satisfied unless the named script installed it and recorded its provenance in
# the base, so a component cannot be quietly skipped by deleting a line here.
#
# Format: <name>  <script>  <one-line note>
#
# niri is here because it is packaged nowhere. Not Debian 12 or 13, not Ubuntu
# 24.04/25.04/26.04, not Fedora, and niri's own GitHub releases attach no binary:
# the only asset on v26.04 is a vendored source tarball. fetch_niri.sh therefore
# verifies that tarball against the SHA-256 upstream publishes and builds it
# offline from its vendored dependency set. That is upstream's own artefact, not
# a third party's package, which is a stronger guarantee than an .deb from an
# unknown build server.
#
# quickshell is deliberately NOT here. Debian packages it in sid, and it is
# installed as an ordinary pinned package, which is why the base is sid.
niri  scripts/fetch_niri.sh  Built from niri's own hash-pinned vendored release bundle; no official binary exists.
